<h2>4. Two ways to read the free energy</h2>

<p>
The free energy admits a second decomposition that is often more useful for
computation. Starting from its definition,
</p>

$$
F(q,y) = \int q(\theta)\,\ln\frac&#123;p(y,\theta)&#125;&#123;q(\theta)&#125;\,d\theta
       = \underbrace&#123;\mathbb&#123;E&#125;_q[\ln p(y,\theta)]&#125;_&#123;\text&#123;expected log-joint&#125;&#125;
       + \underbrace&#123;\mathrm&#123;H&#125;[q]&#125;_&#123;\text&#123;entropy of &#125;q&#125;.
$$

<p>
Maximizing $F$ trades two pressures:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>The <strong>expected log-joint</strong> $\mathbb&#123;E&#125;_q[\ln p(y,\theta)]$ pulls $q$
  toward regions where the model thinks the data are likely, where high prior meets high
  likelihood. It is a "fit" term.</li>
  <li>The <strong>entropy</strong> $\mathrm&#123;H&#125;[q] = -\int q\ln q$ pushes $q$ to spread
  out. It is a "don't be overconfident" term.</li>
</ul>

<p>
Equivalently, and this is the form people optimize in practice,
$F(q,y) = \mathbb&#123;E&#125;_q[\ln p(y\mid\theta)] - \mathrm&#123;KL&#125;[q\Vert p(\theta)]$:
fit the data, but stay close to the prior.
</p>

<p>
The two contributions appear side by side as you change $q$. Watch
the trade-off: shrinking $\sigma_q$ raises the fit term (if $\mu_q$ is in the right
place) but lowers the entropy. The optimum balances them.
</p>

